-- X6 (S1-PRELUDE (a), #2155) — the interface method named `println` is DECLARED
-- and never called, and `main` is an ordinary Unit IO action.  Nothing in this
-- source mentions `println` at a call site at all, so by S1-PRELUDE (a) it must
-- behave exactly as it would if the method were named `printX`: two warnings
-- (the prelude shadow) and a program that prints `hi`.
--
-- What it pins: the driver's composite-main AUTO-PRINT wrap (`main = <e>` →
-- `main = println <e>`, driver/main_autoprint.mdk) must NOT fire on a Unit main.
-- Its gate `shouldAutoPrintMain` reads `mainTypeIsUnit ()`, which reads
-- `mainSchemeRef` — and on the `check`/LSP/playground path (diagnostics.mdk's
-- `analyzeFrom`) that ref was filled by NOBODY, because only the EMIT driver's
-- `elaborateModules` ever wrote it.  So `mainTypeIsUnit ()` answered False for
-- every main, the wrap fired here, and the synthesized `println` — which in THIS
-- module denotes the interface method, exactly as S1-PRELUDE (a) says it must —
-- demanded `Ifc Unit`.  `medaka check` reported `No impl of Ifc for Unit` AT NO
-- LOCATION (rendered as 1:0, the interface decl it has nothing to do with) on a
-- program with no error in it.  The wrap being invisible on an unshadowed
-- `println` (`Display Unit` exists, so its obligation is discharged silently) is
-- why this cell needs the shadow to see the defect at all.
interface Ifc c where
  println : c -> Int

impl Ifc Int where
  println v = v

main = putStr "hi\n"
